 {"id": "DB-GAP-054", "title": "Embedding cache stores 4096-dim vectors as JSON text (~88 KB each) \u2014 6.4 GB in the coding-hermes namespace alone", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "type": null, "created_at": "2026-09-23T14:45:19Z", "created_by": null, "description": null, "acceptance_criteria": null, "source": null, "reasoning": "MEASURED 2026-09-23 (disk audit \u2014 direct JSONL + file inspection, not the index): ~/duckbrain/namespaces/coding-hermes/ is 6,669 MB total, of which .embeddings/openai_qwen_qwen3-embedding-8b is 6,425 MB across 74,945 files; that namespace's actual JSONL memory data is 194 MB and git is 51 MB. hermes-memory shows the same shape at smaller scale \u2014 12 MB of data vs 487 MB of cache (6,286 files). Each entry is the src/embedding/cache.ts CachedEmbedding record serialized with JSON.stringify(vector): a 4096-float vector at full float64 text precision (~21 bytes per number) is ~88 KB on disk, where float32 binary is 16 KB \u2014 a 5.5x inflation, and one file (one inode) per vector. src/embedding/cache.ts already documents vectors as \"derivable artifacts, not source of truth\", keyed by sha256(modelId + contentHash), which is what makes a format change safe and rebuildable. ACCEPTANCE: (1) cache entries read/write without a full-vector JSON text encode \u2014 binary float32 body (length-prefixed buffer plus a small JSON header, or a per-model packed segment file); (2) a one-time migration converts existing entries in place, or the cache is rebuilt per model via the embeddings rebuild CLI; (3) a RED check proves a 4096-dim round-trip returns the identical vector within float32 tolerance and that searches still hit cached entries for unchanged content; (4) measured coding-hermes cache size drops from ~6.4 GB to <= 1.5 GB with the entry count unchanged; (5) the cache-format note in src/embedding/cache.ts and any docs quoting the JSON layout are updated.", "complexity": 3, "updated_at": "2026-09-23T14:45:19Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "ee2461caccf6a611a906bd4b9b47a54df0e299d25e42dbdf1c143bfa1a7086c2"}}
 {"id": "DB-GAP-055", "title": "Partition rotation is one file per few writes \u2014 77,598 segment files in the scheduler namespace, 19,810 in coding-hermes", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "type": null, "created_at": "2026-09-23T14:45:47Z", "created_by": null, "description": null, "acceptance_criteria": null, "source": null, "reasoning": "MEASURED 2026-09-23 (disk audit): two namespaces carry a granularity the storage module's own bound forbids. The scheduler namespace holds 77,598 *.jsonl partition files for 179,818 live records (2.7 records/file); coding-hermes holds 19,810 files for 245,074 records. scheduler/event/2026-09 alone is 40,028 files and scheduler/config/2026-09 is 16,410; coding-hermes/config/2026-08 sits at 10,001 files \u2014 i.e. it reached the 10000.jsonl boundary where padStart(4) stops padding and lexicographic sorting returned an existing name, the exact class documented in the segment-hygiene notes. Cost is not only inode count: at 4 KB block granularity 77,598 files waste ~310 MB and 19,810 files ~79 MB of block slack, git history for the scheduler namespace is 336 MB on top of 296 MB of live record bytes, and every backup/S3 push walks those directories. src/storage/jsonl.ts already declares a 1000-line / 1 MB segment bound and (patched) numeric ordering with no name reuse \u2014 the writer is not honoring the bound. ACCEPTANCE: (1) a segment is rolled only at the declared bound (>= 1000 lines or >= 1 MB), never one file per write \u2014 proven by a test that appends N records and asserts ceil(N/1000) files, not N; (2) numeric (not lexicographic) ordering with a never-reuse-existing-name guard, including across the 9999->10000 boundary; (3) a one-time consolidation pass rewrites over-fragmented month partitions into bounded segments (open FDs closed, JSONL records preserved byte-for-byte in order, no key or id loss) with a before/after file-count + record-count proof per partition; (4) after the pass, coding-hermes drops from 19,810 to under 300 files and scheduler from 77,598 to under 1,000, with the fsck-style record count unchanged; (5) rollback is a git revert in the namespace repo plus the pre-pass tarball.", "complexity": 4, "updated_at": "2026-09-23T14:45:47Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "22459143de73b45754934c0dbb7aba4999a9eb6987e27301da356037084258ae"}}
 {"id": "DB-GAP-056", "title": "Writes accept empty payloads \u2014 60,925 records (33.9% of the scheduler namespace) are literally '{}' from a broken client", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "type": null, "created_at": "2026-09-23T14:45:47Z", "created_by": null, "description": null, "acceptance_criteria": null, "source": null, "reasoning": "MEASURED 2026-09-23 (disk audit of the scheduler namespace): 60,925 of 179,818 live records (33.9%) have an EMPTY payload \u2014 embedding_text is literally \"{}\" with attributes {}. They are not from a real writer: every one carries author \"true@duckbrain.local\" (61,116 records total under that author), which identifies the broken space-form CLI invocation era where the content argument never bound. Volume by day: 09-02 6,379 / 09-03 17,228 / 09-04 31,003, then a few hundred per day until the last occurrence on 09-19 (450); nothing since, so the writer is already stopped. They land on high-traffic fleet keys \u2014 /fleet/projects/eduos.dexdat.com.co/status (1,959), /fleet/summary (1,629), /fleet/namespaces (1,172), /fleet/namespaces/coding-hermes/status (1,168) and 804 apiece on six eduos.dexdat.com.co tick keys \u2014 inflating record counts, recall history and every future compaction, though the newest entry on each of those keys is still real content (checked; no masking). ACCEPTANCE: (1) remember/HTTP write rejects or hard-warns on an empty or \"{}\" embedding_text/content at the schema layer, with a test asserting the write is refused and nothing lands on disk; (2) an empty-content counter is exposed on the health/status surface so a recurrence is visible without a disk audit; (3) the historical 60,925 records are physically removed by a reviewable maintenance pass (rebuild the affected partitions WITHOUT the empty rows, then rebuild the DuckDB index from JSONL) or, if auditable tombstones are preferred, tombstoned and then squashed \u2014 in both cases with a before/after record count per partition, a pre-pass 